  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1118 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nSurface high pressure once again prevails today with an upper\nridge centered over the Ohio River Valley. To the west in the\nnorthern Rockies, a shortwave trough is currently ejecting into\nthe northern Plains ahead of another trough off of the west\nCoast. Mostly clear skies will continue with highs remaining in\nthe 60s and low 70s through this afternoon. Surface dewpoints\nhave been critically underestimated by this morning's guidance\nwith most sites reporting at or below 35 degrees. Therefore,\nchanges were made to account for lower dewpoints in the forecast,\nleading to lower relative humidity values in the low to mid 20s.\n\nLarge scale ridging is forecast to continue through the remainder\nof this week as both troughs circumnavigate the current ridging\ninto Canada. However, the area of surface high pressure will\nshift far enough east through Friday to bring more of a southerly\ncomponent to surface winds across the region. Warmer temperatures\nwill follow as a consequence with highs returning to the 70s\nareawide starting Thursday and lasting into this weekend with\nlows climbing back to the 50s.\n\nBy Saturday, the troughing to the west will become stretched out\ninto a long jet streak over the Rockies and northern Plains. An\narea of enhanced vorticity with a lagging wave to the west of the\nprimary jet streak is forecast to swing south along the southern\nperiphery of the broader trough Saturday, ejecting into the\ncentral Plains. Little in terms of lee cyclogenesis is expected\nas a cold front from an earlier trough will displace the\nbaroclinic zone too far to the east, but the trough will help to\nkick the front farther south and east. By Sunday, the front will\nreach the Mid-South with enough prefrontal moisture advection to\nbring precipitation chances back into the forecast. Both the\nECWMF and GFS show areas of MUCAPE approaching 500 J/kg, but\ndisplaced from the frontal zone, leading to low QPF values. So,\nit appears that scattered showers and thunderstorms are possible,\nbut drought-breaking rainfall isn't.\n\nThe front will pass Sunday afternoon and into Monday morning,\nbringing cooler temperatures back into the forecast. NBM\nprobabilities show about 40% - 50% of lows dropping back below\nfreezing starting Tuesday morning, especially across West\nTennessee and northeastern Arkansas. So, a cool and dry forecast\nis expected beyond Sunday to end the period.",
